interrupt": {"active": true, "reason": "Patient presents with severe leukocytosis (57,500/mm^3) with 35% blasts, indicating potential acute leukemia, and symptoms such as malaise, weakness, night sweats, headache, and blurry vision, which may suggest CNS involvement or other critical conditions requiring immediate attention.", "required_actions": ["Initiate immediate consultation with a hematologist/oncologist", "Order urgent CNS imaging (e.g., CT or MRI of the head) to rule out CNS involvement", "Start infection prophylaxis and consider empiric antibiotic therapy due to the risk of infection in the context of severe leukocytosis and potential immunocompromise", "Monitor patient closely for signs of tumor lysis syndrome and other complications associated with acute leukemia"]}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": ""}, "turn_budget_used": 0, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.0, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.0, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_vignette": "A 57-year-old man presents to the emergency department with several days of malaise, weakness, and night sweats. Today, he experienced a headache with blurry vision, thus prompting his presentation. The patient has a history of diabetes and is followed closely by an endocrinologist. Otherwise, he has lost 10 pounds over the past month. His temperature is 100°F (37.8°C), blood pressure is 122/90 mmHg, pulse is 84/min, respirations are 16/min, and oxygen saturation is 99% on room air. Physical exam is notable for a thin man but is otherwise unremarkable. His visual acuity is 20/100 in both eyes, though he says that he normally has 20/20 vision. His gait is mildly ataxic as well, but his neurological exam is otherwise nonfocal. Laboratory studies are ordered as seen below.\n\nHemoglobin: 10 g/dL\nHematocrit: 31%\nLeukocyte count: 57,500/mm^3 with 35% blasts\nPlatelet count: 109,000/mm^3\n\nSerum:\nNa+: 139 mEq/L\nCl-: 100